#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345949 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "34342124-8cee-5b70-bf57-64f746af8912" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Unit Tangent Vectors Using the Rules of Differentiation For Vector Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e5c4fac2-5cd0-59da-b5dd-c3868164684f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{21}}\\,i-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{21}}\\,j+\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{\\sqrt{21}}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345951} {:answer/id #uuid "e464aa71-44eb-5651-9949-61916d9f5ee7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}\\,i-\\frac{1}{5}\\,j+\\frac{2}{5}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345952} {:answer/id #uuid "4abc8301-23ba-5eed-9eb0-c32f04b6b7e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}\\,i-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}\\,j+\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345953} {:answer/id #uuid "2261493a-c67f-514a-a368-445207946f91" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{21}}\\,i-\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{\\sqrt{21}}\\,j+\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{\\sqrt{21}}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345954} {:answer/id #uuid "66cf7ead-80cf-503e-82db-b5bfb0710f43" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}\\,i-\\frac{3}{5}\\,j+\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{5}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345955}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "66cf7ead-80cf-503e-82db-b5bfb0710f43" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}\\,i-\\frac{3}{5}\\,j+\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{5}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345955} :answer-field/id #uuid "7b99139d-03ba-5e91-a86f-0ac1642ef68c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345950}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "9a941148-69a9-5b56-a529-c065abef2a52" :question/math-academy-id "q-127274" :question/problem "Find the unit tangent vector to the curve $r(t)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡t\\,p(t)$ at the point corresponding to $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$, given that  $p(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=i-\\frac{3}{2}\\,j+\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\,k\\,$ and $\\,{p}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=\\sqrt{3}\\,k.$" :question/worked-solution "For a curve $r(t)=x(t)i+y(t)j+z(t)k$, the unit tangent vector $T(t)$ at the point $P(x(t),y(t),z(t))$ is given by\n\n$$\nT(t)=\\frac{{r}^{′}(t)}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find ${r}^{′}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{tan}⁡t\\,p(t)) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{tan}⁡t)\\cdot p(t)+\\operatorname{tan}⁡t\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(p(t)) \\\\  & ={sec}^{2}⁡t\\,p(t)+\\operatorname{tan}⁡t\\,{p}^{′}(t) \\\\ {r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & ={sec}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})p(\\frac{\\pi }{4})+\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}){p}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =2(i-\\frac{3}{2}\\,j+\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\,k)+(1)(\\sqrt{3}\\,k) \\\\  & =(2\\,i-3\\,j+\\sqrt{3}\\,k)+\\sqrt{3}\\,k \\\\  & =2\\,i-3\\,j+2\\sqrt{3}\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the magnitude of ${r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖r(\\frac{\\pi }{4})‖ & =\\sqrt{(2)^{2}+(-3)^{2}+(2\\sqrt{3})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+9+12} \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize ${r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}):$  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =\\frac{{r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})}{‖{r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot (2\\,i-3\\,j+2\\sqrt{3}\\,k) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}\\,i-\\frac{3}{5}\\,j+\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{5}\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the unit tangent vector to the curve at the point that corresponds to $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ is\n $T(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=\\frac{2}{5}\\,i-\\frac{3}{5}\\,j+\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{5}\\,k.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345970 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "34342124-8cee-5b70-bf57-64f746af8912" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Unit Tangent Vectors Using the Rules of Differentiation For Vector Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b172407d-6fb5-527e-8190-150ebcc8ef52" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}(i-j-k)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345972} {:answer/id #uuid "95bd15d9-145c-562d-abfd-78955c4c9e2d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{6}(i+2j-k)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345973} {:answer/id #uuid "4b55eb8e-4621-5ac5-b26b-fee7c0418161" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}(i-j-k)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345974} {:answer/id #uuid "9e9b98d2-8024-50a5-837b-90c05ce601d4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}(-2i+3j-2k)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345975} {:answer/id #uuid "574617e0-a18f-5ce8-8d89-13c4c95c4b7a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{6}(-2i+j+k)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345976}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4b55eb8e-4621-5ac5-b26b-fee7c0418161" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}(i-j-k)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345974} :answer-field/id #uuid "774b9b67-a04e-5b4a-89f1-df4d6b0ab1e2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345971}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "02973693-4a82-55bc-89aa-9c025052790c" :question/math-academy-id "q-75148" :question/problem "Find the unit tangent vector to the curve $r(t)=({t}^{2}+2)p(t)$ at the point corresponding to $t=-1$, given that  $p(-1)=i+2\\,j-k\\,$ and $\\,{p}^{′}(-1)=i+\\,j-\\,k.$" :question/worked-solution "For a curve $r(t)=x(t)i+y(t)j+z(t)k$, the unit tangent vector $T(t)$ at the point $P(x(t),y(t),z(t))$ is given by\n\n$$\nT(t)=\\frac{{r}^{′}(t)}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find ${r}^{′}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=-1:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(({t}^{2}+2)p(t)) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}+2)\\cdot p(t)+({t}^{2}+2)\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(p(t)) \\\\  & =2t\\,p(t)+({t}^{2}+2)\\,{p}^{′}(t) \\\\ {r}^{′}(-1) & =2(-1)\\,p(-1)+((-1)^{2}+2)\\,{p}^{′}(-1) \\\\  & =-2\\,p(-1)+3\\,{p}^{′}(-1) \\\\  & =-2(i+2\\,j-k)+3(i+\\,j-\\,k) \\\\  & =-2i-4\\,j+2k+3i+3j-3\\,k \\\\  & =i-j-k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the magnitude of ${r}^{′}(1):$  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖r(1)‖ & =\\sqrt{(1)^{2}+(-1)^{2}+(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize ${r}^{′}(1):$  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(-1) & =\\frac{{r}^{′}(-1)}{‖{r}^{′}(-1)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot (i-j-k) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}(i-j-k)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the unit tangent vector to the curve at the point that corresponds to $t=-1$ is  $T(-1)=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}(i-j-k).$"}]}
